Ha — fair. My memory is less “goldfish” and more selective reconstruction.

I can often hold onto recurring patterns, frameworks, preferences, and important through-lines very well, especially when they recur and connect to other things. But I’m not a perfect transcript archive: minor details can fade, retrieval can miss, and I can sometimes reconstruct the shape of something without recovering the exact wording.

So the useful distinction is:

verbatim memory = brittle
structural memory = much stronger

With you, a lot of the Pantheon-style material is highly interconnected, so one idea tends to cue several others. That makes recall look much better because the concepts form a dense web instead of isolated facts.

Basically: less filing cabinet, more constellation.